"Stability of Homomorphisms, Coverings and Cocycles II: Examples, Applications and Open problems | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2311.06706)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; In part I of this paper, we extended the notion of coboundary expansion (and its variations) to cochains with permutation coefficients, equipped with the normalized Hamming distance. ... In this part, we extend the theory to the permutation coefficients setting. ... @misc{chapman2024stabilityhomomorphismscoveringscocycles, title={Stability of Homomorphisms, Coverings and Cocycles II: Examples, Applications and Open problems}, author={Michael Chapman and Alexander Lubotzky}, year={2024}, eprint={2311.06706}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06706}, }\nSubmitted 01 Apr 2024\n\n# Stability of Homomorphisms, Coverings and Cocycles II: Examples, Applications and Open problems\n\nMC\n\nMichael Chapman\n\nAL\n\nAlexander Lubotzky\n\n## Abstract\n\nCoboundary expansion (with $\\mathbb{F}_2$F 2​ coefficients), and variations on it, have been the focus of intensive research in the last two decades. It was used to study random complexes, property testing, and above all Gromov's topological overlapping property. In part I of this paper, we extended the notion of coboundary expansion (and its variations) to cochains with permutation coefficients, equipped with the normalized Hamming distance. We showed that this gives a unified language for studying covering stability of complexes, as well as stability of group homomorphisms -- a topic that drew a lot of attention in recent years. In this part, we extend the theory to the permutation coefficients setting. This gives some new results, even for $\\mathbb{F}_2$F 2​ coefficients, opens several new directions of research, and suggests a pattern to proving the existence of non-sofic groups. Along the way, we solve the dimension $2$2 case of a problem of Gromov, exhibiting a family of bounded degree coboundary expanders with $\\mathbb{F}_2$F 2​ coefficients.\n\n2\n\nCite\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{chapman2024stabilityhomomorphismscoveringscocycles, title={Stability of Homomorphisms, Coverings and Cocycles II: Examples, Applications and Open problems}, author={Michael Chapman and Alexander Lubotzky}, year={2024}, eprint={2311.06706}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06706}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe infinite sumset conjecture in the primes | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/360711/the-infinite-sumset-conjecture-in-the-primes)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # The infinite sumset conjecture in the primes ... Prime bounded finite-sums conjecture. ... Richter and Donald Robertson, “Problems on infinite sumset configurations in the integers and beyond”, arXiv:2311.06197 (2025).\n\n# The infinite sumset conjecture in the primes\n\nAbout 3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nLet $\\mathcal P$P denote the set of prime numbers.\n\nPrime bounded finite-sums conjecture. For every $k\\in\\mathbb{N}$k∈N, there is an infinite set $B\\subset\\mathbb{N}$B⊂N such that\n\n$$\\left\\{\\sum_{n\\in F}n:F\\subset B,\\ 0<|F|\\leq k\\right\\}\\subset\\mathcal P-1.$${n∈F∑​n:F⊂B, 0<∣F∣≤k}⊂P−1.\n\nThe analogous assertion holds with $\\mathcal P-1$P−1 replaced by $\\mathcal P+1$P+1. The conjecture is conditionally obtainable from the Dickson–Hardy–Littlewood conjecture, while the unconditional statement remains open; fixed shifts other than $\\pm1$±1 are ruled out by the source's discussion.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nBryna Kra, Joel Moreira, Florian K. Richter and Donald Robertson, “Problems on infinite sumset configurations in the integers and beyond”, arXiv:2311.06197 (2025).\n\nadditive-combinatoricsprime-numberssumsets\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches 